---
import Layout from '../layouts/doc.astro';
import { ThemeAppMock } from '../components/theme-app-mock';
import CodeBlock from '../components/code-block.astro';
---

<Layout title="Themes">

<h1>Themes</h1>

<p>Same components, different world. <strong>Pick one in the sidebar.</strong></p>

<div class="theme-showcase">
  <!-- Static visual reference: it must remain available without JavaScript. -->
  <ThemeAppMock />
</div>

<h2>Available themes</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Theme</th><th>Light</th><th>Dark</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Solace</td><td>Warm paper, cool ink — equal-weight signals and one vivid blue for action</td><td>Warm paper marks on cool, near-black ink</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Porcelain</td><td>Near-white paper, cool graphite ink, balanced signals</td><td>Cool light ink on a near-black page</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Warm earthen paper, warm-grey ink, quiet low-chroma signals</td><td>Warm ink on a warm-black page</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Marigold</td><td>Pure-grey paper and ink, with deliberately loud bright signals</td><td>Loud signals on black</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Eucalyptus</td><td>Cool-blue paper, blue-black ink, vivid high-chroma signals</td><td>Blue ink on a blue-black page</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Every theme supplies 30 palette values and inherits the same 68 semantic names. Components consume 27 values through that semantic layer; <code>--tone-8</code> through <code>--tone-10</code> are reserved for neutral-ramp compatibility and future deep-ink roles.</p>

<p>Choose a theme with the CLI. It writes the semantic layer and selected palette directly into the CSS file you choose during setup.</p>

<CodeBlock code={`npx @beaket/ui init --theme solace
# Later, switch the saved project to another theme
npx @beaket/ui theme --theme tobacco`} lang="bash" />

<h2>Dark mode</h2>

<p>Every theme ships with a dark twin. Once the CLI has written the tokens, dark mode works automatically — no flags, no extra imports, no toggle configuration required.</p>

<h3>How it works</h3>

<p>The CSS written by the CLI contains both light and dark values. Dark mode activates via <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code>, following the user's OS setting. The semantic layer never changes — shadows and knockouts auto-flip through token references.</p>

<h2>Docs preview controls</h2>

<p>The theme picker in this documentation site is a local preview implementation, not an <code>@beaket/ui</code> CSS API. It reads <code>theme</code> from the URL and <code>beaket-theme</code> / <code>beaket-color-scheme</code> from this site's <code>localStorage</code>, then injects the generated token values onto the document before paint. Those keys affect this documentation site only; importing a package palette does not read them.</p>

<p>For consumer-controlled color schemes, own that CSS in your application. The package currently ships OS-driven <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> behavior only; it does not provide a class, data attribute, or <code>localStorage</code> toggle contract.</p>

</Layout>

<style>
  :global(.main) {
    max-width: none;
  }
  .theme-showcase {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
</style>
